\section*{Constant-factor hardness of uniform sparsest cut}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 117; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that Uniform Sparsest Cut is NP-hard to approximate by any fixed factor greater than one, for rational capacities and unit pair demands.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For an undirected graph whose capacities are nonnegative rational numbers, with each pair of distinct vertices assigned demand one, set
\[
 \Phi(G)=\min_{\varnothing\ne S\subsetneq V}
 \frac{\sum_{u\in S,v\notin S}c_{uv}}{|S|\,|V\setminus S|}.
\]
For each fixed $C>1$, the claimed polynomial-time reduction from $3$CNF satisfiability outputs $G$ and positive rational $a$ with
\[
 \varphi\text{ satisfiable}\Rightarrow\Phi(G)\le a,
 \qquad\varphi\text{ unsatisfiable}\Rightarrow\Phi(G)>Ca.
\]
All output bit lengths are polynomial in formula size for fixed $C$; the demands remain exactly one.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 117; 2026-09-24), an additive tower produces a short explicit direction list with robust quadratic support. A mixing parity test and deterministic correction enforce almost-quadratic consistency; the resulting gap is converted to a graph while preserving uniform demands.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 117; 2026-09-24).

